  title: "Switches and Network Interfaces — How Data Finds a Path Across the Cluster",
  date: "2026-09-20",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 040: GPUs handle many kinds of work, while custom chips can use less energy when their design closely matches the task.

Lesson 041: Compilers and kernels turn model calculations into chip instructions, so better software helps the accelerator spend less time waiting.

Lesson 042: A faster accelerator saves money only if the extra work it completes is worth its higher hourly price.

Lesson 043: Scale-up tightly connects a group of accelerators, while scale-out connects servers and racks into a larger cluster.

Lesson 044: Collective operations let accelerators share or combine results, and the group may have to wait for those exchanges to finish.

The big idea

For two GPUs in different servers to share results, data needs a way out of one server, a path across the network and a way into the other.

A network interface is the server's doorway. A switch is the junction that directs traffic between doorways. A fast doorway helps only if the rest of the path can carry the traffic.

1. Follow one piece of data

Start with all-reduce from Lesson 044: each GPU contributes numbers, the numbers are combined, and every GPU receives the combined result. During training, those numbers can be gradients—instructions for how the model's weights should change.

The collective library, software that coordinates this exchange, divides the data into chunks. Here is a common path for one chunk going to another server:

- It leaves GPU memory over PCI Express, or PCIe: an internal connection between devices in the server.
- It reaches a network interface card, or NIC: hardware that sends and receives network data.
- The NIC divides the transfer into packets: small pieces of data with information about where they should go.
- A cable carries those packets to a switch.
- The switch selects an outgoing port, meaning a connection to the next link.
- After one or more switches, the destination NIC receives the packets and puts the data into memory.

InfiniBand is one type of cluster network. Its server interface is often called a host channel adapter, or HCA. For this lesson, remember that NICs and HCAs perform the endpoint role: connecting a server to the network.

One all-reduce can require many such transfers.

2. How the NIC avoids extra copying

A NIC does more than connect a cable. It manages packets, holds work in queues and moves data between memory and its network ports.

Normally, extra copies of data can consume CPU time and memory bandwidth. Remote direct memory access, or RDMA, allows network hardware to read or write memory that software has prepared for access, without the CPU copying each chunk.

NVIDIA's GPUDirect RDMA extends this idea to GPU memory through a supported PCIe connection. Data can avoid an intermediate copy into the server's main memory.

But “direct” still means traveling over hardware. The arrangement of the PCIe connections—called the topology—matters. Some paths are more efficient than others, and unsupported arrangements may not work.

Removing a copy can reduce delay and free bandwidth. It cannot make the NIC's network port transmit faster than its own limit.

3. Why packets wait inside a switch

A switch can forward traffic through many ports at once. Its internal switching fabric is the hardware that moves packets from incoming ports to outgoing ports.

Suppose two incoming streams each want to send at 400 gigabits per second through the same 400-gigabit outgoing port. Together they demand twice what that port can carry.

Packets must wait in a buffer, a temporary holding area. If the demand continues, the queue grows. The network must slow senders or deal with dropped packets, depending on how it is designed.

